::CW_U06_L03_Q01::Readers cannot tell what the main character wants. Which revision should come first?{
~A. Add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not clarify the character's goal.
=B. Add an action or line of dialogue that reveals the character's goal.#Correct. Motivation becomes clearer when readers see what the character wants.
~C. Change the font.#Incorrect. Font does not solve character motivation.
~D. Delete the title.#Incorrect. The issue is character motivation, not title.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q02::A scene meant to be suspenseful reaches the reveal in one sentence. What is the best revision?{
~A. Remove suspense entirely.#Incorrect. This works against the scene's purpose.
~B. Add an unrelated character.#Incorrect. This does not control pacing.
~C. Change all names.#Incorrect. Names do not slow the reveal.
=D. Add action beats and sensory details before the reveal.#Correct. These slow pacing and build tension.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q03::Which revision best clarifies conflict?{
=A. Show what each character wants and why those wants cannot both happen.#Correct. Conflict becomes clear when competing wants are visible.
~B. Add a list of furniture.#Incorrect. Furniture does not create conflict by itself.
~C. Remove the character's choice.#Incorrect. This weakens conflict.
~D. Use a random flashback.#Incorrect. Flashback should be purposeful.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q04::A draft accidentally shifts from first person to third person. What should the writer revise?{
~A. The weather description.#Incorrect. The problem is narration.
~B. The title only.#Incorrect. The title does not fix point of view.
=C. Point of view consistency.#Correct. The writer should control the narrator's position.
~D. The page number.#Incorrect. Page number is irrelevant.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q05::Which explanation best supports portfolio value?{
=A. I revised the final action so readers understand why the character leaves.#Correct. This explains revision and reader effect.
~B. I changed something somewhere.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. The story is nice.#Incorrect. This is unsupported praise.
~D. I typed it again.#Incorrect. Retyping is not craft evidence.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q06::Which story problem requires structure revision?{
~A. One typo in a clear final draft.#Incorrect. That is convention polish.
~B. A purposeful short sentence.#Incorrect. This may be a style choice.
~C. A clear conflict.#Incorrect. Clear conflict is a strength.
=D. The middle event happens before the setup that explains it.#Correct. Sequence problems require structural revision.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q07::A peer says the character's decision feels sudden. Which revision best responds?{
~A. Change the character's eye color.#Incorrect. This does not prepare the decision.
=B. Add a moment showing the character weighing the choice.#Correct. This prepares the decision.
~C. Add a random sound.#Incorrect. A sound helps only if connected to the decision.
~D. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue may help reveal decision.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q08::Which is the best first step before revising a fiction scene?{
~A. Correct every comma before reading the scene.#Incorrect. Diagnose the scene first.
~B. Change the ending randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
=C. Identify the scene's job and main craft problem.#Correct. Diagnosis guides targeted revision.
~D. Delete all setting details.#Incorrect. Setting may be useful.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q09::Which feedback is most useful for narrative revision?{
~A. I like it.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Make it better.#Incorrect. This gives no target.
~C. The story exists.#Incorrect. This is not feedback.
=D. I understand the setting, but I do not know why Mara apologizes in the final scene.#Correct. This identifies a specific motivation issue.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q10::Which revision best improves character development?{
=A. Add a choice that reveals what the character values.#Correct. Character is shown through choices.
~B. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not develop character by itself.
~C. Remove the character's goal.#Incorrect. That weakens development.
~D. Change the file name.#Incorrect. File name is irrelevant.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q11::What should a writer do if feedback asks for more clarity but the mystery is intentional?{
~A. Delete every mysterious detail.#Incorrect. Mystery may be purposeful.
~B. Ignore all feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated.
=C. Clarify necessary motivation while preserving purposeful mystery.#Correct. This balances reader access and intended effect.
~D. Make the scene more confusing.#Incorrect. Confusion weakens reader understanding.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q12::Which revision best controls point of view?{
~A. Add every character's private thoughts in one paragraph.#Incorrect. That creates accidental shifts.
=B. Keep only the narrator's thoughts in a first-person scene.#Correct. This maintains point-of-view consistency.
~C. Remove all pronouns.#Incorrect. This is not a practical POV revision.
~D. Add unrelated description.#Incorrect. Description does not fix POV.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q13::Which change is most likely final polishing rather than revision?{
=A. Correcting a misspelled word after the scene's structure is clear.#Correct. This is a convention polish.
~B. Adding motivation before the ending.#Incorrect. This is revision.
~C. Reordering events for clarity.#Incorrect. This is structural revision.
~D. Adding conflict to a flat scene.#Incorrect. This is revision.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q14::A scene has no clear conflict. Which revision is strongest?{
~A. Add a random object.#Incorrect. Objects matter only if tied to conflict.
~B. Remove every choice.#Incorrect. This weakens conflict.
~C. Change margins.#Incorrect. Margins do not create conflict.
=D. Add a difficult choice with consequences.#Correct. Conflict often comes from pressure and consequence.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q15::Which portfolio explanation is weakest?{
~A. I revised the pacing so suspense builds before the door opens.#Incorrect. This is specific.
~B. I clarified the character's motivation through an action beat.#Incorrect. This is specific.
=C. I made it better.#Correct. This is too vague to show portfolio value.
~D. I fixed the point of view so readers stay with Mara's experience.#Incorrect. This explains revision.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q16::Which revision best improves pacing in a rushed emotional scene?{
~A. Remove the emotional moment.#Incorrect. This avoids the problem.
=B. Add a brief action beat before the character responds.#Correct. This slows the moment and lets emotion register.
~C. Add unrelated backstory.#Incorrect. Backstory may slow but can distract.
~D. Change every verb randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be targeted.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q17::Which answer best identifies a motivation problem?{
~A. Readers know the character wants forgiveness.#Incorrect. That is clear motivation.
~B. The punctuation is correct.#Incorrect. This is not motivation.
~C. The setting is vivid.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
=D. Readers cannot tell why the character refuses to speak.#Correct. The reason for action is unclear.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q18::Which revision best preserves voice while using feedback?{
=A. Clarify the confusing action but keep the narrator's informal word choice where it supports voice.#Correct. This improves clarity without erasing voice.
~B. Rewrite the whole story in a formal research style.#Incorrect. That may erase fiction voice.
~C. Ignore the confusing action.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies a real issue.
~D. Remove the narrator.#Incorrect. This is extreme.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q19::A story's events are out of order by accident. What is the best revision target?{
~A. Font size.#Incorrect. Font does not reorder events.
=B. Sequence and structure.#Correct. Event order is a structural issue.
~C. Favorite color.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. Title decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration does not fix sequence.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q20::Which sentence best explains a narrative revision?{
~A. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This lacks craft reason.
~B. It is better now.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. I added the note under the door so readers understand why Ellis leaves without speaking.#Correct. This names revision and reader effect.
~D. I made edits.#Incorrect. This gives no detail.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q21::Which craft area is most connected to "who tells the story"?{
=A. Point of view.#Correct. Point of view controls who tells or perceives the story.
~B. Font color.#Incorrect. Font color does not determine narrator.
~C. Page count.#Incorrect. Page count is length.
~D. Spelling only.#Incorrect. Spelling is conventions.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q22::Which revision best develops fiction character?{
~A. Add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather only helps if tied to character.
~B. Remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Conflict often develops character.
=C. Add a meaningful choice under pressure.#Correct. Choices reveal character.
~D. Change the title punctuation only.#Incorrect. This does not develop character.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q23::Which issue should be revised before portfolio submission?{
~A. A purposeful action beat slows suspense.#Incorrect. That may be effective.
~B. Dialogue reveals character clearly.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
~C. Motivation is easy to follow.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
=D. The central conflict is still unclear.#Correct. Portfolio-ready fiction needs clear conflict.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q24::Which answer best matches the problem to revision?{
~A. Problem: unclear POV; Revision: add random weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not fix POV.
=B. Problem: unclear POV; Revision: control whose thoughts are included.#Correct. This correctly matches issue and fix.
~C. Problem: rushed scene; Revision: delete character goal.#Incorrect. This weakens scene.
~D. Problem: weak conflict; Revision: correct one typo only.#Incorrect. Typo correction does not build conflict.
}

::CW_U06_L03_Q25::Which response best shows Lesson 3 mastery?{
=A. I can diagnose a narrative craft problem, make a targeted revision, and explain the reader effect.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill.
~B. I can change random details.#Incorrect. Revision must be targeted.
~C. I can ignore character motivation.#Incorrect. Motivation matters.
~D. I can polish spelling while leaving the story confusing.#Incorrect. Major craft problems must be fixed.
}
